tures like Horner's syndrome or radiographic mass strongly argues against apical lu…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.491], ["B2", 0.346], ["B1.1", 0.3]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.557], ["B2", 0.341], ["B1.1", 0.279]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.213], ["B2", 0.146], ["B1.1", 0.113]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute instability markers (airway compromise, shock, uncontrolled hemorrhage) or time-critical syndrome patterns (e.g., stroke, STEMI) requiring immediate intervention. Cerebral infarction (C) is possible but symptoms are subacute (weeks) and not sudden-onset, which reduces urgency for emergent stroke protocol.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1) posterior (0.213) < 0.75 and dangerous alternatives (B1.1 with posterior 0.113) remain unresolved. Tree depth (2) < max (3), and discriminators (e.g., imaging, EMG) are still available to refine differentials. Management would differ between neoplastic (B1) and vascular (B2) branches, so actionable_parent does not apply."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.68, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.14575506915508077, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'weakness', 'Peripheral sensory neuropathy', 'onset', 'brachial plexopathy', 'Brachial plexus neuropathy', 'vascular disease', 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Abnormality of the brachial nerve plexus'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.549}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Vascular Disease", "posterior": 0.146, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 3, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The patient's subacute neurological deficit with constitutional symptoms (weight loss, heavy smoking) provides moderate…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 4, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nVascular Disease vs Brachial Plexus Disorders:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n\nVascular Disease vs Metastatic Neoplasm to Brachial Plexus:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal cortical gyration\n\nVascular Disease vs Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nBrachial Plexus Disorders vs Metastatic Neoplasm to Brachial Plexus:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, diminished sensation, electric shock-like sensation, intense pain, loss of feeling\n\nBrachial Plexus Disorders vs Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, loss of feeling in the arm, numbness of the shoulder, arm, or hand\n\nMetastatic Neoplasm to Brachial Plexus vs Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nRelated to Vascular Disease: angiodysplasia, arterial calcification of infancy, arterial disorder\n\nRelated to Brachial Plexus Disorders: amyotrophic neuralgia, brachial plexus neoplasm, brachial plexus neuritis\n\nRelated to Metastatic Neoplasm to Brachial Plexus: brachial plexus neuropathy, nerve plexus neoplasm\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Brachial Plexus Disorders\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Metastatic Neoplasm to Brachial Plexus"}
